 ==== Priority of conditions
 
-- The if-statement with reset "if (rst)" is first, so the reset works independent from en as long as its enabeled (en=1).
+- The if-statement with reset "if (rst)" is first, so the reset works independently of en as long as it is enabled (en=1).
 - Only after that statement comes "else if (en)": Without enable, the counter holds its value. 
 This way, no latch is implemented, but a flipflop that holds its value.
 
 ==== Synchronous Reset
 
-- The reset only apply on the edge trigger, due to its presence inside of the "always @(posedge clk)" syntax. 
+- The reset only applies on the clock edge, due to its presence inside the "always @(posedge clk)" block. 
   This is called a synchronous reset. 
-- The alternative would be an asynchrounous reset, which applies immediately: "always @(posedge clk or posedge rst)"
+- The alternative would be an asynchronous reset, which applies immediately: "always @(posedge clk or posedge rst)"
 
 ==== The conditional operator
 
-- The syntax "(q == MOD - 1) ? 0 : q + 1" reads like in the programming language C: When the counter is on 9, he becomes 0 else he is incremented by one.
-- Downsides the same: Is the counter 0, he becomes 9 else a decrement by one.
-- In hardware the syntax "? :" results in a multiplexer. Yosys creates 6 $_MUX_-cells.
+- The syntax "(q == MOD - 1) ? 0 : q + 1" reads like in the programming language C: when the counter is at 9, it becomes 0, otherwise it is incremented by one.
+- Counting down works the same way: if the counter is 0, it becomes 9, otherwise it is decremented by one.
+- In hardware the syntax "? :" results in a multiplexer. Yosys creates 6 $_MUX_ cells.
 
